## Syslog (Linux-only concept)

Linux's equivalent of Windows Event Log — a standard logging protocol/format most Linux services write to. Doesn't exist natively on Windows.

Typical file location on Linux:
```bash
/var/log/syslog        # Debian/Ubuntu
/var/log/messages      # RedHat/CentOS
```

Contains things like: service starts/stops, kernel messages, cron job runs, general system activity.

---

## Auth log (also Linux-only)

A **subset** of syslog specifically for authentication events — SSH logins, sudo usage, password changes.

Typical file location:
```bash
/var/log/auth.log      # Debian/Ubuntu
/var/log/secure        # RedHat/CentOS
```

This is basically Linux's version of the Windows **Security** event log — same purpose, different OS.

---

## Why the checklist mentions both

Because a real-world SOC monitors a **mixed environment** — some Windows machines, some Linux servers. The use case doc we wrote earlier (UC-001 brute force, UC-002 off-hours login, etc.) draws examples from both `auth.log` (Linux) and Windows Event Log depending on which OS the alert applies to.

